Yes — that is the stronger formulation.

If enough of the generative pattern survives, the lost object does not need to survive as an object. You only need a system capable of projecting the pattern back into form.

That gives three layers:

pattern → interpreter → projection

The “internet” could disappear materially, yet survive as a sufficiently complete generative description: protocols, topology, social functions, examples, interfaces, failure modes, economic roles, cultural traces. A future civilization could reconstruct an internet-like system from the pattern even if not one original router survived.

And the stars point toward an extreme version of the same principle.

In theory, you could use astronomical structures as an encoding substrate in several ways: deliberately modulated stellar light, artificial occultations/transits, spectral signatures, pulsar-like timing patterns, or even geometric relations between conspicuous stars used as an enormous addressing system. The stars themselves could be memory, clock, beacon, and coordinate system at once.

The deeper trick is that you would not necessarily encode the entire payload literally. You could encode a bootstrap.

Something like:

recognize artificial pattern → recover mathematics → recover notation → recover decoding rules → recover compressed knowledge → rebuild tools → decode increasingly richer layers

Then a tiny unmistakably artificial signal could unfold into something enormously larger once the receiver acquires the tools specified by the earlier layers.

That is your “predict the tools and external of the future internal” idea pushed to its limit: encode not only information, but instructions for creating the interpreter capable of recovering the rest.

Almost like a cosmic seed.

And “summon their projections” is exactly right. Storage does not have to preserve the original phenomenon. It can preserve enough constraints that the phenomenon becomes the most stable reconstruction.

A musical score doesn’t contain sound.
DNA doesn’t contain an adult organism.
Equations don’t contain an orbit.
A seed doesn’t contain a tree at tree scale.

They contain instructions plus invariants that recruit an environment to recreate the missing scale.

So the ultimate preservation architecture may be:

Do not preserve the cathedral. Preserve a pattern that can teach future matter how to build cathedrals.

And if you can make the pattern substrate-independent enough, then stars, stone, DNA, radio, language, mathematics, machines—or things we haven’t invented yet—become merely different instruments capable of playing the same surviving score.